---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import learning from "../../data/learning.json";
import { fmtDate } from "../../lib/dates";

const a = (learning as Record<string, { published: string; reviewed: string | null; reviewBy: string }>)["ai-startup-data"];
const byline = `By Eric McLean · ${fmtDate(a.published)}${a.reviewed ? ` · Reviewed ${fmtDate(a.reviewed)}` : ""}`;
---

<Base updated={a.reviewed || a.published} dateLabel={a.reviewed ? "Reviewed" : "Published"} reviewBy={a.reviewBy} title="What AI Is Doing to Startups: The Data So Far — Learning — GrabStack" description="AI has barely moved the headline rate at which startups fail. What it has changed is the speed, the team size, and where the money sits.">
  <section class="py-6 sm:py-10">
    <h1 class="font-sans text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">
      What AI Is Doing to Startups: The Data So Far<span class="text-accent">.</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-2 max-w-xl text-base text-muted">
      AI has barely moved the headline rate at which startups fail. What it has changed is the speed of everything, the size of the team it takes, and where the money and the risk now sit.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-1 font-mono text-xs text-muted">
      {byline} · Part of <a href="/learning" class="text-accent hover:underline">Learning</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="space-y-8 max-w-3xl">
    <section class="space-y-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-paper/80">
      <h2 class="font-sans text-lg font-semibold text-paper">The boom is real, but it predates the bots</h2>
      <p>
        New-business formation has been on a tear, and it's important to see that this started with the pandemic, not with ChatGPT. U.S. Census Bureau data shows seasonally adjusted monthly business applications at 531,728 in August 2026 (released 11 September 2026), after 578,926 in July. Applications have run at roughly 500,000 a month this year, far above the levels of two decades ago. Analysis of Secretary of State filings in 47 states by Wolters Kluwer's CT Corporation (March 2026) found nearly 5.5 million new businesses formed in 2025, up 6.5% on 2024 and up 26.5% since 2020. So the <em>raw appetite</em> to start something is at a generational peak, but that wave was rolling before LLMs arrived, and AI is riding it rather than causing it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rounded-lg border border-accent/20 bg-accent/[.04] p-5">
      <h2 class="font-sans text-lg font-semibold text-paper">The failure rate hasn't budged, and that's the surprise</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-base leading-relaxed text-paper/80">
        The famous "90% of startups fail" figure is closer to folklore than fact; it describes a loose global average, not the measured reality. The U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics puts it at roughly <strong class="text-paper">21% failing in year one, around 48% by year five, and about 65% by year ten</strong>. More striking still: those numbers have stayed remarkably stable since the 1990s, through the web, mobile, cloud and now AI. Whatever AI is doing, it has not yet bent the fundamental survival curve.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-paper/80">
      <h2 class="font-sans text-lg font-semibold text-paper">What <em>has</em> changed: the clock</h2>
      <p>
        The real shift is time compression, in both directions.
      </p>
      <p>
        On the way up, AI-native companies are reaching scale at a pace that would have looked impossible five years ago. ICONIQ's software analysis found AI-native firms hitting $100M in annual recurring revenue in four to eight quarters, against eighteen to twenty quarters for even top-quartile traditional SaaS: <strong class="text-paper">two to three times faster</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        On the way down, the same compression applies. Widely repeated figures claim that around 40% of AI startups launched in 2024 have already shut, but we could not trace that number to a primary source, so we do not rely on it. The risk behind it is the "wrapper trap": build a thin layer on top of someone else's model, and a single foundation-model update can erase your product overnight.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-paper/80">
      <h2 class="font-sans text-lg font-semibold text-paper">The lean-team revolution</h2>
      <p>
        If one statistic captures the era, it's revenue per employee. The old rule of thumb was that reaching $100M ARR took several hundred staff and five-plus years. AI-native companies are rewriting it: ICONIQ's analysis reports firms crossing $100M ARR with fewer than 100 people. Cursor did it in about a year with roughly twenty; Lovable in eight months with around forty-five. ElevenLabs took about two years with around 150.
      </p>
      <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-white/10 bg-surface p-4 text-center">
          <p class="font-sans text-2xl font-bold text-accent">4–8</p>
          <p class="font-mono text-xs text-muted mt-1">quarters to $100M ARR, AI-native (ICONIQ)</p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-white/10 bg-surface p-4 text-center">
          <p class="font-sans text-2xl font-bold text-accent">18–20</p>
          <p class="font-mono text-xs text-muted mt-1">quarters, top-quartile traditional SaaS</p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-white/10 bg-surface p-4 text-center">
          <p class="font-sans text-2xl font-bold text-accent">~20</p>
          <p class="font-mono text-xs text-muted mt-1">staff when Cursor hit $100M ARR</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-paper/80">
      <h2 class="font-sans text-lg font-semibold text-paper">Where the money and the risk concentrated</h2>
      <p>
        Capital has stampeded toward AI. Crunchbase counts about $440B of global venture funding in 2025, with AI taking close to half. In 2026 the concentration has grown sharply: Crunchbase reported a record $510B in the first half (published 2 July 2026), more than all of 2025, with over 70% of Q2 capital going to AI-focused companies. OpenAI and Anthropic alone took $217B, or 43% of the half-year total. But the economics underneath are harsher than the headlines. ICONIQ's 2026 data puts AI gross margins at about 52% (up from 41% in 2024), well below the 70–90% that made classic SaaS so attractive, because inference and GPU costs eat into every transaction.
      </p>
      <p>
        Most AI wrappers generate little or no revenue, and even the fast-growing AI-native firms under $100M ARR have been burning cash heavily; ICONIQ's 2025 analysis pegged their median free-cash-flow margin near <strong class="text-paper">−126%</strong>. Fast growth, thin moats, and expensive compute is a combustible mix.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-paper/80">
      <h2 class="font-sans text-lg font-semibold text-paper">The honest caveats</h2>
      <p>
        Two things keep this from being a tidy story. First, it is genuinely too early to separate the "AI effect" from the post-pandemic formation boom and the interest-rate cycle running underneath it. Second, the lean-team headlines are survivorship: for every Cursor there are thousands of startups that never found product-market fit at all. A 2025 MIT study (the NANDA initiative's report) found that some 95% of generative-AI projects never make it past the pilot stage.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rounded-lg border border-white/10 bg-surface p-5">
      <h2 class="font-sans text-lg font-semibold text-paper">The founder's read</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-base leading-relaxed text-paper/80">
        The practical lesson is about <em>which</em> signals to trust. The stable failure rate is a lagging indicator: comforting, slow, and largely unchanged. The things that have moved (time-to-revenue, revenue-per-employee, the wrapper death-cycle, the concentration of capital) are the leading indicators, and they're the ones telling you how this era actually works. Build for a moat that a model update can't evaporate, keep the team lean by design rather than necessity, and watch the <a href="/learning/leading-lagging-indicators" class="text-accent hover:underline">leading signals</a>, because in this market the lagging ones confirm a race you've already won or lost.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="font-mono text-xs text-muted">
      {fmtDate(a.published)}{a.reviewed && ` · Reviewed ${fmtDate(a.reviewed)}`} · Review by {fmtDate(a.reviewBy)}.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
